# {{ .input_project_name }}

A [Kedro](https://docs.kedro.org) project that deploys to Databricks as an asset bundle.
Every Kedro node becomes a task in a Databricks job, so the same pipeline code runs
locally and on a cluster with no branching.

It ships with an example machine learning pipeline built on the familiar
[Iris dataset](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/uciml/iris), using only PySpark and Kedro:

* `split` — splits the data into train and test sets using a configurable ratio
* `make_predictions` — runs a 1-nearest-neighbour classifier
* `report_accuracy` — logs the accuracy of those predictions

[Transcoding](https://docs.kedro.org/en/stable/data/data_catalog_yaml_examples.html#read-the-same-file-using-two-different-datasets)
converts the Spark DataFrames into pandas DataFrames after the split, so the later nodes
work with pandas while the read and the split stay in Spark.

## Code structure

```
{{template `project_name_alphanumeric_underscore` .}}/
├── databricks.yml              <- Bundle root: name, variables, artifacts, sync, includes, and the dev_qa/uat/prod targets.
├── pyproject.toml              <- Project metadata and dependencies. [project.scripts] declares the entry point the Databricks tasks call.
├── uv.lock                     <- Locked dependency versions for reproducible installs (generated by uv).
│
├── conf/
│   ├── base/                   <- Shared Kedro config: catalog.yml, parameters.yml.
│   ├── local/                  <- Your own credentials and overrides. Never committed.
│   ├── dev_qa/ uat/ prod/
│   │                           <- Per-target config. catalog.yml points datasets at that
│   │                              target's storage; databricks.yml holds the job overrides
│   │                              that `kedro databricks bundle` merges into resources/.
│   └── logging.yml
│
├── data/                       <- Kedro's layered data directories. Real data lives in the workspace, not here.
│
├── src/{{template `project_name_alphanumeric_underscore` .}}/
│   ├── pipeline_registry.py    <- Discovers pipelines under pipelines/.
│   ├── settings.py             <- Config loader setup, including the `databricks` config pattern.
│   └── pipelines/iris/         <- The example pipeline: nodes.py and pipeline.py.
│
├── resources/                  <- Databricks resource definitions. See resources/README.md.
│
├── monitoring/                 <- Notebooks for the opt-in quality monitoring resource.
│
├── notebooks/                  <- Ad-hoc notebooks.
└── tests/
```

## Getting started locally

Dependencies are managed by [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/):

```bash
uv sync --all-extras        # Install all dependencies
uv run pytest tests         # Run tests
uv run kedro registry list  # -> __default__, iris
```

`conf/base/catalog.yml` points at `/dbfs/FileStore/...`, which only exists on Databricks.
To run the pipeline on your own machine, add a `catalog.yml` under `conf/local/` with
local paths and run:

```bash
kedro run --env local
```

You can also explore the pipeline graph with `kedro viz`.

## Deploying to Databricks

### 1. Authenticate

Install the [databricks CLI]({{ template `generate_doc_link` (map (pair "path" "dev-tools/cli/databricks-cli.html")) }})
(v{{template `cli_version` .}} or newer) and configure a profile:

```bash
databricks configure --host <your workspace host>
```

Check that the three `targets` in `databricks.yml` (dev_qa, uat, prod) name the right workspaces for your
organisation before your first deploy.

### 2. Deploy

```bash
databricks bundle validate --target dev_qa
databricks bundle deploy  --target dev_qa
```

`databricks.yml` declares an `artifacts` entry that runs `kedro package` as part of the
deploy, so the wheel in `dist/` is always rebuilt from your current source. The `sync`
block ships `conf/` to the workspace, which is what the tasks pass to Kedro as
`--conf-source`.

### 3. Run

```bash
databricks bundle run {{template `project_name_alphanumeric_underscore` .}} --target dev_qa
```

The `dev_qa` target uses `mode: development`, so the deployed job is prefixed with your user
name and its schedule is paused. `uat` and `prod` deploy the schedule as written.

## Changing the pipeline

The job definitions under `resources/` are generated from the Kedro pipelines. After
editing a pipeline, regenerate them:

```bash
kedro databricks bundle --overwrite --env dev_qa
```

Then review `git diff resources/`. See [resources/README.md](./resources/README.md) for
what is generated, what is hand-maintained, and where to change cluster size, schedules
and permissions.

### A note on the entry point

The Databricks tasks invoke the project by console-script name, and that name is derived
from `tool.kedro.project_name` by lowercasing it and replacing every non-letter with a
dash. Because digits become dashes too, a project named `my-project2` gets the script
name `my-project`. `pyproject.toml` already declares the correct name under
`[project.scripts]`; if you rename it, the deployed tasks will fail to start until
`resources/` is regenerated to match.
